\f1\fs22 Sarah dialled Hayley, invited her to an immediate meeting, and almost suggested she bring her cutlass. She waited dispassionately for Hayley to attend, taking her eyes from Chip for not a moment.\
Chip did not sit. The visitor chairs\'97deficient in both size and sturdiness\'97would not survive the luxury. He leaned casually on the only filing cabinet in the office in the same way a cement plant might lean on a bus shelter during an earthquake.\
Hayley arrived in four minutes.\
\'93Ah, Mr Stockwell,\'94 Hayley said.\
\'93Ma\'92am.\'94\
Hayley swivelled a chair so she could keep Chip in view without appearing to disrespect Sarah\'92s presence, and sat.\
Sarah and Hayley waited him out in silence. It made him uncomfortable; he was used to people being in fear and awe around him, and he had spent his career in an organisation where nobody would shut up.\
\'93Five discs\'97\'94 Chip started.\
\'93We call them kebabs here, Mr Stockwell,\'94 Sarah said.\
\'93That term does not adequately describe how dangerous they are, Ms Jacobson.\'94\
\'93Have you ever had a kebab from a food truck at four o\'92clock in the morning, Mr Stockwell?\'94\
\'93I am never on my feet at four o\'92clock in the morning, Ms Jacobson,\'94 Chip said and was instantly aware of the pain in his shoes.\
\'93You should try being pregnant.\'94\
\'93As I was saying: Mr Damagetus wants a pentagram of dis\'97kebabs. This is very dangerous. You already know what happens when just one of these things spins too fast.\'94\
Sarah shuffled some papers on her desk, moved her computer mouse around and clicked a few times. Chip was in no position to see what was on the screen. \'93Two kebabs are more stable than one, correct? And we\'92re currently spinning them at slower speeds for the same outcome.\'94\
\'93For now.\'94\
Sarah thought of the patterns in her dreams and shuddered. They had only become more vivid as Little Clinton grew inside her. \'93Something or someone is trying to communicate with us, don\'92t you agree?\'94\
Chip put his tablet on the filing cabinet and left it alone; he had rehearsed with the tablet and understood that the moment he brought up a graph he would become a man with a graph.\
\'93You see patterns,\'94 he said. \'93I see weapons development.\'94\
Hayley sat forward, crossed her legs and placed a finger to her lips. Chip scanned the room for the places listening devices and cameras might be.\
\'93Project Kebab is cleaning up the signals we are already receiving, as well as looking for more,\'94 Sarah said. \'93There are no plans to harness the kebabs\'92 instabilities and, so far, all the evidence says they become more stable when we use them in concert.\'94\
\'93Fridge Force knows one thing,\'94 Chip said. \'93Decades of incident reports, and it comes down to a single line: stasis fields must never be allowed to interact with one another. It fits on a beer coaster. Two units in a building, you shield them. Two units in a room\'85 you don\'92t put two units in a room.\'94\
Sarah nodded deliberately. \'93I hear you, Mr Stockwell. And I need your continued expertise and vigilance. But let me assure you, we have not and will not develop the kebabs to blow shit up.\'94\
\'93Says the employee of an arms dealer,\'94 Chip replied.\
\'93All three of us are employed by Shakti Enterprises, Mr Stockwell,\'94 Hayley said, running her hand across her neck in a throat-cutting gesture. \'93And all three of us are committed to ensuring the safety of the research.\'94\
\'93Mr Stoat wants five kebabs because a man with a beard recited him a poem.\'94 Chip would throw Mr Stoat under a kebab cart at any opportunity. \
\'93The Sages report to me,\'94 Sarah said, and then, slower: \'93All of Johnny\'92s reports also report to me.\'94\
\'93Ms Jacobson. You hired me for my expertise. One thing I can guard you against is another Rotherhithe-style incident,\'94 he said.\
\'93You\'92ve said Rotherhithe before.\'94\
Chip considered his words carefully. \'93Rotherhithe effectively slid into the Thames.\'94\
\'93If I recall,\'94 Hayley started. \'93There was a flood.\'94\
\'93There was what stasis scientists call a multidimensionally colocated resonant concurrency event,\'94 Chip stated. \'93It\'92s what can happen when you tax around with Gyges fabric.\'94\
\'93Fabric,\'94 Sarah checked.\
\'93For want of a better term, yes,\'94 Chip said, \'93because, mathematically, there are multiple dimensions apparently 
\f2\i weaving
\f1\i0  through it. The physics is still theoretical, but the observed effects are quite real.\'94\
Sarah started, \'93So there was one of these multidimensional\'97\'94\
But Chip overrode her with, \'93Multidimensionally colocated resonant concurrency event.\'94\
\'93You know that absolutely stinks like you are trying to hide something,\'94 Sarah accused.\
Chip shrugged. \'93It took Fridge Force nearly a year of intense analysis to discover that, as Rotherhithe rose up and fell into the Thames, a resonance threshold was attained as three kebabs were spinning at well above safe speeds.\'94\
\'93So some lab in Rotherhithe went too far,\'94 Sarah dismissed. \'93Just give us the data and we won\'92t make the same mistakes.\'94\
Chip sighed. \'93The discs were not in a lab. And they were not in Rotherhithe\'97at least not in any common three-dimensional sense.\'94\
Sarah winced as she sat up too fast for Little Clinton. \'93Where were they?\'94\
Chip laboured to make his mouth say, \'93They were being used at a quasi-religious rave party.\'94\
\'93Where?\'94\
\'93Goa.\'94\
\'93As in Goa, India?\'94 Sarah checked. \'93As in the place where old DJs with Zimmer frames go to retire.\'94\
Chip hoped he could get his eyebrows to nod without moving his head. He almost completely failed.\
\'93So, kebabs can destroy whole neighbourhoods seven and a half thousand kilometres away.\'94\
Chip remained silent and let Sarah draw her own conclusions.\
\'93Seriously?\'94 Hayley challenged. \'93That much destruction?\'94\
\'93Sarah already knows the effect of a kebab spinning at the speed of a coin toss,\'94 Chip said.\
Sarah shuddered.\
\'93So let me explain how the danger escalates,\'94 Chip said. \'93There\'92s a simple formula: two to the power of 
\f2\i n
\f1\i0 , minus 
\f2\i n
\f1\i0 , minus one, where 
\f2\i n 
\f1\i0 is the number of kebabs. Every pair can resonate. Every trio can resonate. Every group of four can resonate. Then there\'92s the whole lot together. With five kebabs, that gives you twenty-six different ways the fields can gang up on ordinary space.\'94\
Sarah stared at him. \'93You rehearsed that.\'94\
\'93Yes. Each combination has its own geometry and phase relationships. Some wash out; some reinforce each other. Hold enough of them in coherence and the whole system starts behaving like one field.\'94\
\'93And neighbourhoods thousands of kilometres away get demolished,\'94 Hayley said.\
Chip nodded. \'93With three kebabs.\'94 Then Chip decided he\'92d attempt a joke. \'93And that\'92s before you add garlic sauce.\'94\
\'93I hate garlic sauce,\'94 Sarah said. \'93Especially if it\'92s anywhere near a kebab.\'94\
\'93And Johnny wants five,\'94 Hayley remarked.\
Chip shrugged. \'93The 
\f2\i Sages
\f1\i0  want five. Mr Stoat just wants to claim the sparkiest idea as his own.\'94\
\'93Then just present your evidence,\'94 Sarah said. \'93Johnny will be claiming he contributed to that too before he\'92s finished reading the first abstract.\'94\
\'93I can\'92t. It\'92s classified.\'94\
\'93I\'92m sure Eloise can help you negotiate those obstacles,\'94 Sarah said.\
Chip just stared at her.\
Sarah returned his stare with the serene professionalism of someone prepared to let another person make a terrible mistake for several seconds. \'93I want to stop Mr Stoat from making a potentially catastrophic mistake.\'94\
\'93So heed my professional advice, and tell Mr Stoat to stand down.\'94\
Hayley leant forward in her chair. \'93Just heed your advice, Chip? After all we\'92ve been through? You\'92ve tried to hold out on me before, so my sweet little detective soul wants evidence.\'94\
\'93I haven\'92t forgotten how you and Eloise trapped me into this,\'94 Chip said icily.\
Hayley thought for a moment. \'93You know what? I\'92m sorry, Chip. I apologise for that. But admit it: you must have enjoyed the view.\'94\
\'93I\'92m not fond of heights,\'94 Chip admitted flatly.\
\'93No, I suppose you aren\'92t.\'94 Hayley looked Chip over. \'93I imagine lugging that great carcass of yours up a flight of stairs requires a civil engineering consultant.\'94\
Chip refused to be embarrassed about it. \'93I can go out at night and not get mugged.\'94\
\'93Civil engineering required for that too,\'94 Hayley said. \'93But, look. We were talking about my faults. I\'92ve treated you unfairly, intimidated you\'85\'94\
\'93Nothing intimidates me.\'94\
\'93I suppose not,\'94 Hayley agreed. \'93But what about your previous employers? They mustn\'92t be happy about you defecting and joining Shakti Enterprises.\'94\
\'93I didn\'92t defect.\'94 Chip was stating fact as he saw it.\
\'93Are you sure? I mean, it would be easy to find out. Wouldn\'92t it? I could call a hover-copt to take you downtown, and you could visit your Fridge Force friends and settle it for all of us. That would settle it\'97wouldn\'92t it, Chip\'97if you landed on the highest hover pad on the Fridge Force building downtown, in a Shakti Enterprises hover-copt?\'94\
Chip ground his teeth and said nothing.\
Hayley checked Sarah\'92s posture; she wasn\'92t about to intervene. \'93I mean,\'94 Hayley continued, \'93I imagine they\'92ve already sentenced you 
\f2\i in absentia
\f1\i0  to 300 years inside a freezer inside a stasis prison.\'94\
Chip took a moment. \'93Something like that.\'94\
\'93So you\'92ll supply the documentation we need?\'94 Sarah asked. \'93If it\'92s as you say it is, I\'92ll stop Johnny eating any more kebabs.\'94\
Chip worked his jaw and winced as he pushed his weight off the filing cabinet. \'93Good day, Ms Jacobson.\'94 He lumbered out of the office, on feet that were clearly causing him some discomfort.
